.\" Manual page for scrutiny-agent(1).
.\" Format with:  man ./scrutiny-agent.1  (macOS)   or   man -l scrutiny-agent.1  (Linux)
.TH SCRUTINY-AGENT 1 "2026-09-21" "scrutiny-agent 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scrutiny-agent \- remote git, filesystem and LSP backend for Scrutiny
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scrutiny-agent
.RB [ \-\-rpc\-stdio ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B scrutiny-agent
.B \-\-version
.br
.B scrutiny-agent
.BR \-\-help " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scrutiny-agent
is the process a client spawns on a remote machine, next to the source
trees it wants to work with, and drives over the client's own stdio
transport
.RB ( ssh ,
.BR "ssh \-J" ,
.BR tsh ,
.BR "kubectl exec" ,
a local shell, and so on).
It speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 with LSP-style
.B Content-Length
framing on standard input and standard output, and answers git,
filesystem, language-server, indexing and cache requests against the
working trees on the host.
There is no network listener: stdio is the only channel.
.PP
Its primary client is Scrutiny, a macOS GitHub pull-request review
application, whose remote clones run entirely through this agent.
An Emacs client ships in the same repository and uses the agent to
carry
.BR find-file ,
saving, dired, magit and eglot over a single connection.
.PP
The agent is not interactive.
Run by hand it waits on standard input until EOF and then exits.
Standard output carries protocol frames only; every diagnostic goes to
standard error or to the log file named by
.BR \-\-log .
.PP
Policy (which directories may be read, whether writes are allowed,
which git subcommands
.B git.exec
may run) is set at startup from the command line and/or a
configuration file, and cannot be changed by a client over the wire.
The effective policy is reported by
.B meta.capabilities
and printed to standard error at startup.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are read left to right; a configuration file named by
.B \-\-config
is applied at the point it appears, and later options add to or
override it.
Every option except
.BR \-\-rpc\-stdio ,
.B \-\-version
and
.B \-\-help
has a configuration-file key of the same name without the leading
dashes; see
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5).
Repeatable options and file keys accumulate.
.TP
.B \-\-rpc\-stdio
Serve the JSON-RPC protocol on stdin/stdout.
This is what a bare invocation does too; the flag is accepted so that
a client's exec line is stable and says what it does.
It has one real effect: it is never mistaken for an askpass prompt
(see
.BR "ASKPASS MODE" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Read options from the file
.IR path ;
see
.B CONFIGURATION FILE
below and
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5)
for the format and the keys.
A relative
.I path
is anchored under
.BR $HOME .
An unreadable file is a warning on standard error, not an error, so an
exec line that carries every setting itself still starts.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow\-root " path"
Add
.I path
to the filesystem sandbox.
Every path given to an
.B fs.*
method is anchored under
.B $HOME
if relative, resolved through symlinks with
.BR realpath (3),
and must then lie inside one of the allowed roots; anything else
answers
.B PERMISSION_DENIED
(1005) before the kernel is touched.
Relative roots anchor under
.BR $HOME .
Repeatable.
A root that does not resolve is dropped with a warning rather than
denying everything under it.
If no root survives, the sandbox is
.B $HOME
itself; if
.B $HOME
is unset or does not resolve either, every filesystem call is denied.
Roots are printed to standard error at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-write
Serve the filesystem write methods
.RB ( fs.writeFile ,
.BR fs.mkdir ,
.BR fs.delete ,
.BR fs.rename ,
.BR fs.copy ,
.BR fs.chmod ).
Off by default: the agent is read-only, the write methods are not
advertised in
.BR meta.hello ,
and
.B meta.capabilities
reports
.BR "writable: false" .
Writes are confined to the same allowed roots;
.B \-\-allow\-write
widens what may be done to a path, never which paths are reachable.
.TP
.BI \-\-git\-exec " subcommand"
Allow one git
.I subcommand
(for example
.BR log ,
.BR status ,
.BR push )
through the
.B git.exec
method.
Repeatable.
.B git.exec
is off, and not advertised, until at least one subcommand is allowed
by this option or by
.BR \-\-git\-exec\-preset .
.TP
.BI \-\-git\-exec\-preset " name"
Allow a named set of subcommands through
.BR git.exec :
.B read\-only
(inspection only) or
.B magit
(inspection plus the working operations magit needs, a grant close to
.B git
itself).
The sets are listed in
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5).
An unknown
.I name
is a warning and grants nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-git\-exec\-config " key"
Permit
.B git.exec
requests to pass
.BI "\-c " key = value
before the subcommand, in addition to the built-in set of formatting
and local-behaviour keys.
.I key
is a full
.B section.name
or a bare section name, which permits every key in that section.
Never add a key that makes git run a program; the built-in set and the
reasoning are in
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5).
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " path"
Append a log to
.IR path .
Without this option nothing is logged.
When the file reaches 8 MiB it is renamed to
.IB path .1
(a single backup) and a fresh file is started.
A relative
.I path
is used as given, relative to the agent's working directory; a client
that writes a configuration file normally names an absolute path or
one inside its install directory.
Credential values are redacted from the log at every level, because
.B logs.tail
streams the log back to the client.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " level"
One of
.BR off ,
.BR error ,
.BR warn ,
.BR info ,
.BR debug ,
.BR trace .
The default once
.B \-\-log
is given is
.BR info .
An unrecognised
.I level
is treated as
.BR off ,
which disables logging entirely.
At
.B trace
request parameters are logged in full (except credentials); below it
content-bearing parameters are redacted and only paths, queries, ids,
lanes, sizes, timings and errors are kept.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.RI ` "version" " proto " N '
(the agent version and the wire protocol version it serves) on
standard output and exit 0.
Clients run this right after installing a binary: it proves at once
that the file executes on this architecture, that dynamic linking
resolved, and that the intended version was installed, before a single
frame is exchanged.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary and exit 0.
.PP
An unknown option is reported on standard error and ignored; the
agent still starts and never writes a diagnostic to standard output.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Every option above except
.BR \-\-rpc\-stdio ,
.B \-\-version
and
.B \-\-help
can also be set in the file named by
.BR \-\-config ,
as
.B key = value
lines whose keys are the option names without the leading dashes.
A file and the command line feed the same table inside the agent and
apply in the order they are read.
The format, every key, the git subcommand presets and the permitted
.B \-c
keys are documented in
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5).
A small file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# policy for this host
allow-root = src             # relative: ~/src
allow-root = /srv/repos
git-exec-preset = read-only
log = /home/me/.scrutiny-agent/agent.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
Clients such as Scrutiny and the Emacs client write this file beside
the agent binary as
.IB installDir /scrutiny-agent.conf
at bootstrap and start the agent with
.B \-\-config
pointing at it, so the policy lives on the host and every client using
that install directory agrees on it.
.SH OPERATIONS
Requests are JSON-RPC 2.0 objects whose
.B method
is
.IB namespace . operation\c
\&.
Parameters are an object.
The sections below list every method the agent serves, grouped by
namespace.
Unless stated otherwise, a
.B path
parameter is absolute on the host; relative cache and install paths
are anchored under
.BR $HOME .
The wire format, error codes and streaming rules are summarised under
.BR PROTOCOL .
The normative reference is
.I docs/protocol.md
in the source tree.
.SS Session and diagnostics
.TP
.B meta.hello
.RI { clientVersion ", " supportedProtocolVersions [], " frameCap? }"
\(->
.RI { agentVersion ", " protocolVersion ", " frameCap ", " capabilities [] }.
Must be the first request.
.I capabilities
is the exhaustive list of methods this process serves; opt-in
surfaces
.RB ( fs.write* ,
.BR git.exec )
appear only when enabled.
A client that cannot speak the agent's protocol version gets
.B VERSION_MISMATCH
(1006).
.TP
.B meta.capabilities
\(->
.RI { agentVersion ", " protocolVersion ", " outboundIO [], " fileSystemAccess" {}, " languageServers" {}, " network" ", " selftestMethod }.
The security posture of the running process.
.I fileSystemAccess
carries
.I allowedRoots
(the canonical roots this process is running with),
.I writable
and
.I home
(the host's
.BR $HOME ,
which a client needs to expand
.BR ~ ).
.TP
.B meta.stat
\(->
.RI { lanes ", " lspSessions ", " lspTunnels ", " uptimeMs ", " gitParallel ", " logLevel ", " agentVersion }.
Live counters for the operations viewer.
.TP
.B meta.debug
.RI { sleepMs? ", " padBytes? }
\(->
.RI { echoId ", " pad }.
Sleeps (cancellably) and pads its result, to exercise cancellation and
chunking.
.TP
.B logs.tail
.RI { maxBytes? }
\(->
.RI { enabled ", " path? ", " level? ", " bytes? ", " text? }.
The tail (at most 1 MiB) of the
.B \-\-log
file, or
.B enabled: false
when logging is off.
.SS Git, read-only
Each takes
.I path
(the work tree) unless noted.
.TP
.B git.headSha
\(->
.RI { headSha }.
.TP
.B git.repoMetadata
\(->
.RI { path ", " gitDir ", " isBare ", " headSha ", " currentBranch ", " hasUncommittedChanges }.
.TP
.B git.remotes
\(->
.RI { remotes ": [{ " name ", " url ", " pushUrl " }] }."
.TP
.B git.branches
.RI { path ", " local? ", " remote? }
\(->
.RI { branches ": [{ " name ", " refname ", " targetOid ", " isRemote ", " isHead ", " upstream ", " remoteName " }] }."
.TP
.B git.commits
.RI { path ", " branch? ", " limit? }
\(->
.RI { commits ": [...] }"
newest first;
.I limit
defaults to 100.
.TP
.B git.aheadBehind
.RI { path ", " branch }
\(->
.RI { ahead ", " behind }
against the branch's upstream.
.TP
.B git.diffForCommit
.RI { path ", " sha }
\(->
.RI { diffs ": [FileDiff] }."
An unknown
.I sha
yields an empty list.
.TP
.B git.workingTreeDiff
\(->
.RI { diffs }
for index to work tree, including untracked files.
.TP
.B git.stagedDiff
\(->
.RI { diffs }
for HEAD to index.
.TP
.B git.showFile
.RI { path ", " sha ", " file }
\(->
.RI { content }
(null when the file is absent in that commit).
.TP
.B git.diff
.RI { path ", " from ", " to ", " file }
\(->
.RI { diff }
via a
.B git diff
subprocess.
.TP
.B git.isAncestor
.RI { path ", " ancestor ", " descendant }
\(->
.RI { isAncestor }
via
.BR "git merge-base \-\-is\-ancestor" .
.PP
A
.I FileDiff
is
.RI { status ", " oldPath ", " newPath ", " patch ", " hunks ": [{ " oldStart ", " oldLines ", " newStart ", " newLines ", " header ", " lines ": [{ " origin ", " content ", " oldLineNo ", " newLineNo " }] }] }."
.SS Git, mutating and network
These run on the
.B bulk
lane and, where git may prompt for a password, through the credential
broker (see
.BR SECURITY ).
.TP
.B git.checkoutBranch
.RI { path ", " branch }
\(-> {}.
A safe checkout; a refusal answers
.B INTERNAL
with libgit2's reason.
.TP
.B git.clone
.RI { fullName ", " cloneURL ", " installDir ", " authOpId }
\(->
.RI { localPath ", " cloneURL ", " lastFetched }.
Clones into
.IR installDir / fullName
with
.B /
replaced by
.BR _ .
.TP
.B git.fetch
.RI { repoPath ", " authOpId }
\(->
.RI { ok ", " lastFetched }
.RB ( "git fetch \-\-all \-\-prune" ).
.TP
.B git.ensureRepository
Same parameters and result as
.BR git.clone ;
fetches when the clone already exists.
.SS General git (git.exec, opt-in)
.TP
.B git.exec
.RI { repoPath ", " args [], " authOpId? }"
\(->
.RI { exitCode ", " stdout ", " stderr ", " truncated }.
Runs one allowlisted git subcommand in
.IR repoPath ,
with
.B \-\-no\-pager
prepended,
.B GIT_PAGER=cat
and
.B GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0
in the environment.
A non-zero
.I exitCode
is data, not an error (git answers questions with exit codes).
Each output stream is capped at 16 MiB;
.I truncated
says when the cap bit.
The subcommands
.BR fetch ,
.BR pull ,
.BR push ,
.BR ls-remote ,
.B clone
and
.B submodule
go through the credential broker when
.I authOpId
is given.
Runs on the
.B normal
lane by default.
.IP
Before any process starts, the request is checked against the policy
in
.B SECURITY
below; a refusal is
.B PERMISSION_DENIED
(1005) whose message names the offending argument.
Served only when
.B \-\-git\-exec
or
.B \-\-git\-exec\-preset
enabled it.
.SS Filesystem
All paths pass through the sandbox described under
.BR \-\-allow\-root .
.TP
.B fs.readFile
.RI { path ", " base64? ", " stat? }
\(->
.RI { content }
or
.RI { contentBase64 },
plus
.IR size ", " mtime ", " mode
with
.BR "stat: true" .
.TP
.B fs.listDirectory
.RI { path ", " attributes? }
\(->
.RI { path ", " entries ": [{ " name ", " isDir " }] }"
sorted by name, symlinks followed for
.IR isDir .
With
.B attributes: true
each entry also carries
.IR size ", " mtime ", " mode ", " isRegular ", " isSymlink ", " symlinkTarget ", " uid ", " gid ", " readable ", " writable
and the resolved
.IR path .
.TP
.B fs.stat
.RI { path }
\(->
.RI { exists ", " isDir ", " isRegular ", " isSymlink ", " symlinkTarget ", " size ", " mtime ", " mode ", " uid ", " gid ", " readable ", " writable ", " path }
or
.RI { "exists: false" }.
The parent is canonicalised and authorised, then the leaf is
.BR lstat (2)ed,
so a symlink is described rather than resolved away.
.TP
.B fs.statBatch
.RI { paths [] }
\(->
.RI { stats ": [...] }"
in the order asked; a bad entry yields
.B exists: false
(plus
.B denied: true
outside the roots) instead of failing the batch.
.TP
.B fs.selftest
\(->
.RI { probe ", " succeeded ", " errorCode ", " reason? ", " firstBytesReadable ", " sampleSnippet }.
Reads
.I /etc/passwd
through the same resolve-authorise-read path a client request takes,
so it reports this process's real posture.
.SS Filesystem writes (opt-in, \-\-allow\-write)
.TP
.B fs.writeFile
.RI { path ", " content " | " contentBase64 ", " mode? ", " createDirs? }
\(->
.RI { ok ", " size ", " mtime }.
Atomic: temp file beside the target,
.BR fsync (2),
then
.BR rename (2)
over it.
An existing file's mode is kept unless
.I mode
is given.
.TP
.B fs.mkdir
.RI { path ", " parents? }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
.TP
.B fs.delete
.RI { path ", " recursive? }
\(->
.RI { ok ", " removed }.
.TP
.B fs.rename
.RI { from ", " to }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
Both ends are authorised.
.TP
.B fs.copy
.RI { from ", " to ", " overwrite? }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
Both ends are authorised.
.TP
.B fs.chmod
.RI { path ", " mode }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
.PP
Authorising a path that does not exist yet resolves the deepest
existing component and appends the rest, so a symlink inside the roots
cannot become a door out of them.
.SS Language servers, request level
Common parameters:
.I workspacePath
(the project root),
.I language
(an integer: 1 rust, 2 python, 3 javascript, 4 typescript, 5 go,
6 cpp, 7 c, 8 swift),
.I filePath
(absolute on the host, or relative to
.IR workspacePath )
and
.IR fileContent ;
positional methods add
.I line
and
.IR character .
One server session per (workspace, language) is spawned on first use
(rust-analyzer, pylsp, typescript-language-server, gopls, clangd,
sourcekit-lsp, looked up on
.BR PATH ).
Before a server starts the agent prepares the workspace: for C and C++
it finds or generates a
.B compile_commands.json
and passes clangd the compilers it names; for Swift it locates an
Xcode index store when there is one.
A server that is still indexing is given time to warm up before an
empty answer is accepted.
.TP
.B lsp.gotoDefinition
.RI { "position" }
\(->
.RI { locations ", " source ", " note? }.
.I source
is
.B server
or
.BR text :
when no server can run, or it answers nothing, the locations are
ranked declaration-shaped text matches and
.I note
says why.
A missing server is never an error here.
.TP
.B lsp.findReferences
.RI { "position, " includeDeclaration? }
\(->
.RI { locations ", " source ", " note? }.
Same fallback, using whole-word occurrences across the workspace's
files of that language.
.TP
.B lsp.hover
\(->
.RI { hover ": { " contents ", " hasRange ", " range " } | null }."
.TP
.B lsp.documentSymbols
\(->
.RI { symbols }.
.TP
.B lsp.workspaceSymbols
.RI { workspacePath ", " language ", " query }
\(->
.RI { symbols }.
.TP
.B lsp.foldingRange
\(->
.RI { ranges ": [{ " startLine ", " endLine ", " hasKind ", " kind " }] }."
.TP
.B lsp.workspaceInfo
.RI { workspacePath ", " language }
\(->
.RI { language ", " workspacePath ", " serverArguments [], " compileCommandsDir" ", " indexStorePath ", " source ", " crossFileCapable ", " notes [] }.
What the agent found or generated for cross-file answers;
.I source
is one of
.BR found ,
.BR generated ,
.BR package ,
.BR server ,
.BR none ,
and
.I notes
say what is missing and how to fix it.
Starts the server if needed.
.PP
Language-server failures answer
.B LSP_FAILED
(1004); the message carries what to install when the server binary is
absent.
.SS Language servers, raw tunnel
The tunnel pipes bytes verbatim between the client and a server the
agent spawns with its working directory set to the workspace.
The agent neither parses nor reframes LSP traffic; a real LSP client
(eglot, an IDE) speaks its native protocol through it.
.TP
.B lsp.tunnelOpen
.RI { workspacePath ", " language }
\(->
.RI { tunnelId ", " serverPath }.
.B LSP_FAILED
when no server binary is installed.
.TP
.B lsp.tunnelSend
(notification)
.RI { tunnelId ", " data }
with
.I data
base64: client-to-server bytes, written in arrival order.
.TP
.B lsp.tunnelClose
.RI { tunnelId }
\(->
.RI { ok },
idempotent; closes the server's stdin and sends
.BR SIGTERM .
.PP
The agent emits
.B lsp.tunnelRecv
.RI { tunnelId ", " data }
(at most 32 KiB of raw bytes per notification, in stream order) and
.B lsp.tunnelClosed
.RI { tunnelId ", " exitCode ", " reason }.
Back-pressure is bounded in both directions; the client-to-server
queue is capped at 8 MiB.
.SS Indexer
.TP
.B index.create
.RI { workspacePath ", " language ", " cacheDBPath }
\(->
.RI { indexerId }.
A relative
.I cacheDBPath
lands under
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B index.run
.RI { indexerId }
\(->
.RI { filesIndexed ", " definitionsFound ", ... }."
Streams
.B index.progress
.RI { indexerId ", " filePath ", " current ", " total }
notifications, coalesced to roughly one per percent.
Bulk lane.
.TP
.B index.cancel
(notification)
.RI { indexerId }.
.TP
.B index.destroy
.RI { indexerId }
\(->
.RI { ok };
idempotent, refused while running.
.SS HEAD watching
.TP
.B watch.head
.RI { path }
\(->
.RI { watchId }.
Observes the clone's resolved
.I .git/HEAD
file (worktree
.B gitdir:
pointers followed) and emits a debounced
.B watch.headChanged
.RI { watchId }
on checkout, branch switch, rebase or any other rewrite of HEAD.
A plain commit moves the branch ref, not HEAD, and does not fire.
.TP
.B watch.stop
(notification)
.RI { watchId }.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.B cred.provide
.RI { credId ", " value }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
The client's answer to a
.B cred.request
notification;
.B ok
is false for an unknown or expired
.IR credId .
.TP
.B cred.selftest
.RI { authOpId ", " prompt }
\(->
.RI { got ", " askpassExit }.
A full broker, unix-socket and self-exec round trip.
Diagnostic; not advertised.
.SS Diff cache
An SQLite cache on the host keyed by
.RI ( fromSha ", " toSha ", " file ).
Entries are immutable, so there is no invalidation, only pruning.
.TP
.B diffcache.get
.RI { cacheDir ", " fromSha ", " toSha ", " file }
\(->
.RI { hit ", " value? }.
.TP
.B diffcache.put
.RI { cacheDir ", " fromSha ", " toSha ", " file ", " value }
\(->
.RI { ok }.
.TP
.B diffcache.prune
.RI { cacheDir ", " days }
\(->
.RI { removed }.
.SS Notifications sent by the agent
.TP
.B rpc.chunk
.RI { id ", " seq ", " last ", " data }
when a response body exceeds the frame cap.
.TP
.B index.progress
during
.BR index.run .
.TP
.B watch.headChanged
when a watched HEAD changes.
.TP
.B cred.request
.RI { authOpId ", " credId ", " prompt }
when git prompts during a brokered operation.
.TP
.BR lsp.tunnelRecv ", " lsp.tunnelClosed
for an open tunnel.
.SH PROTOCOL
.SS Framing
Each message is
.B Content-Length:
.IR N ,
CRLF, CRLF, then
.I N
bytes of UTF-8 JSON.
The header name is matched case-insensitively.
A header block without
.B Content-Length
(a late login banner, a
.B motd
line) is answered with a null-id
.B INVALID_REQUEST
and skipped; so is a body that is not valid JSON.
Only EOF on standard input ends the session, after which the agent
drains in-flight work, stops watches and tunnels, and exits 0.
Frames are capped at a size negotiated in
.B meta.hello
(default 128 KiB, clamped to 64 to 256 KiB).
A response larger than the cap is streamed as
.B rpc.chunk
notifications followed by a small
.RI { "streamed: true" ", " bytes }
result.
.SS Cancellation
The notification
.B $/cancelRequest
.RI { id }
drops queued work before it starts and asks running work to stop at
its next safe point; a cancelled request answers
.B CANCELLED
(1007).
.SS Priority lanes
Work runs on a pool of 16 threads in three lanes,
.BR interactive ,
.B normal
and
.BR bulk ,
with per-lane caps (normal 8, bulk 2) so a clone or an index run cannot
starve a hover.
The writer also serves higher lanes first at frame boundaries.
Defaults per method:
.BR index.run ,
.BR git.clone ,
.B git.fetch
and
.B git.ensureRepository
are bulk;
.BR meta.debug ,
.BR cred.selftest ,
.B git.exec
and
.B lsp.tunnelOpen
are normal; everything else is interactive.
A request may carry a top-level
.B lane
field to override.
.SS Error codes
.TS
l l l.
1000	INTERNAL	agent-side failure; the message carries the reason
1001	NOT_FOUND	repository, file, path or id missing; unknown method
1002	INVALID_REQUEST	malformed JSON, or missing or mistyped parameters
1003	GIT_FAILED	git subprocess failed; the message carries its stderr
1004	LSP_FAILED	language server unavailable or crashed
1005	PERMISSION_DENIED	path outside the sandbox, or a refused git.exec request
1006	VERSION_MISMATCH	protocol version negotiation failed
1007	CANCELLED	request cancelled by the client
.TE
.PP
The distinction between
.B NOT_FOUND
and
.B PERMISSION_DENIED
is deliberate and load-bearing for clients: the latter is what a
client uses to offer "allow this directory".
.SH SECURITY
.SS Filesystem sandbox
Described under
.BR \-\-allow\-root .
The kernel only ever sees the canonical, symlink-resolved path, which
closes the symlink-swap race between check and use.
.SS Credential broker
Git running under the agent never receives a token in a URL, a config
setting or the environment.
For a brokered operation the agent creates a private unix socket in
.B $TMPDIR
and re-executes itself as
.B GIT_ASKPASS
(see
.BR "ASKPASS MODE" ).
When git prompts, the agent sends a
.B cred.request
notification to the client, which answers with
.BR cred.provide ;
the secret is written to the askpass child and exists on the host only
while git is reading it.
Values are redacted from the log at every level.
.SS git.exec policy
Two checks, and the second is the one that matters:
.IP 1. 3
The subcommand must be on the allowlist built from
.B \-\-git\-exec
and
.BR \-\-git\-exec\-preset .
.IP 2. 3
No argument may be one of git's "run this program for me" options.
A subcommand allowlist alone is not security:
.BR "\-c core.pager=..." ,
.BR "\-c credential.helper=..." ,
.BR "\-c alias.x=!..." ,
.BR \-\-upload\-pack= ,
.BR \-\-receive\-pack= ,
.B \-\-exec\-path=
and friends turn any permitted subcommand into arbitrary code
execution.
Also refused anywhere in the argument list:
.BR \-\-git\-dir ,
.BR \-\-work\-tree ,
.BR \-C ,
.B \-\-namespace
(which would move the repository out from under
.IR repoPath ),
.BR \-\-config\-env ,
.BR \-\-ext\-diff ,
.BR \-\-gpg\-sign= ,
.B \-\-output=
and
.BR \-\-textconv .
.PP
Before the subcommand only
.BR \-\-no\-pager ,
.BR \-\-paginate ,
.BR \-\-no\-replace\-objects ,
.BR \-\-literal\-pathspecs ,
.B \-\-no\-optional\-locks
and
.B \-\-bare
are accepted at all, plus
.BI "\-c " key = value
for allowlisted keys (see
.B \-\-git\-exec\-config
and
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5)).
Repository-local configuration and hooks are the user's own and are
out of scope; the policy prevents the client from injecting them.
.SH ASKPASS MODE
The same binary is also git's askpass helper for the agent's
credential broker.
For a brokered git operation the serving agent creates a private unix
socket, sets
.B SCRUTINY_CRED_SOCK
to its path and
.B GIT_ASKPASS
to its own executable, and runs git.
When git needs a password it runs
.BI "scrutiny-agent '" prompt "'"
and that child connects to the socket, sends the prompt, prints the
secret it receives on standard output for git, and exits.
Any failure exits 1, so git (run with
.BR GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 )
fails authentication cleanly rather than hanging.
.PP
The mode is selected before any option is parsed, by this rule alone:
.B SCRUTINY_CRED_SOCK
is set in the environment and there is exactly one command-line
argument, and that argument is neither
.B \-\-version
nor
.BR \-\-rpc\-stdio .
Nothing else is examined, because git passes the prompt text as an
ordinary argument and it may look like anything.
Two consequences follow.
.IP \(bu 3
In an environment where
.B SCRUTINY_CRED_SOCK
is already set, a bare
.B scrutiny-agent
.I something
would be taken for an askpass prompt.
Passing
.B \-\-rpc\-stdio
(or a second argument) prevents that, which is why clients always
include it.
A normal login shell never has the variable set; only the agent's own
git children do.
.IP \(bu 3
The variable is not a setting.
Setting it by hand does not enable anything in the serving agent, and
an askpass invocation whose socket is missing or refuses the
connection simply exits 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Anchors relative sandbox roots, config paths, cache and install
directories, and is the default sandbox root.
When unset, relative paths anchor under
.I /tmp
and no default sandbox root exists.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the credential broker's unix socket is created.
Kept out of the repository because
.B sun_path
is 108 bytes.
.TP
.B PATH
Where language servers and
.B git
are found.
.TP
.B SCRUTINY_CRED_SOCK
Path of the credential broker's unix socket.
Set by the serving agent on the git processes it spawns, never read by
the serving agent itself; together with a single argument it selects
.BR "ASKPASS MODE" .
.TP
.BR GIT_ASKPASS ", " GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT ", " GIT_PAGER
Set by the agent on the git processes it spawns.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB installDir /scrutiny-agent.conf
The configuration file clients write beside the binary at every
bootstrap and pass with
.BR \-\-config ;
format in
.BR scrutiny-agent.conf (5).
.TP
.IR path ", " path .1
The log file named by
.B \-\-log
and its single rotated backup.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean shutdown (EOF on standard input), after
.B \-\-version
or
.BR \-\-help .
Startup problems (an unknown option, an unreadable config file, a root
that does not resolve) are warnings and do not change the exit status.
In askpass mode, 1 when the secret could not be obtained.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a freshly installed binary:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ scrutiny-agent \-\-version
0.2.0 proto 1
.RE
.fi
.PP
Serve one handshake by hand, read-only, sandboxed to two directories,
with magit-level git access and an info log:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ scrutiny-agent \-\-rpc\-stdio \e
      \-\-allow\-root src \-\-allow\-root /srv/repos \e
      \-\-git\-exec\-preset magit \e
      \-\-log ~/.scrutiny-agent/agent.log \-\-log\-level info
.RE
.fi
.PP
Smoke-test the protocol without a client:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ body='{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"meta.hello",
         "params":{"supportedProtocolVersions":[1]}}'
$ printf 'Content-Length: %d\er\en\er\en%s' ${#body} "$body" \e
      | scrutiny-agent \-\-rpc\-stdio
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run it from a configuration file on the host (the
.B \-\-rpc\-stdio
flag is optional; a bare invocation serves too):
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ scrutiny-agent \-\-config ~/.scrutiny-agent/scrutiny-agent.conf
.RE
.fi
